import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Sleeve Qualification — Full Public Research — LUNA"
  description="Full public report for LUNA Sleeve Qualification Mode A repetition 1: controlled starting state, blinded review, four inference models, provenance tests, latency, failures, and limitations."
>
  <header class="article-header full-hero">
    <div class="shell study-wide">
      <p class="eyebrow">FULL PUBLIC RESEARCH · EXPERIMENT #233</p>
      <h1>Sleeve Qualification:<br />Does Luna Survive a Model Swap?</h1>
      <p class="study-deck">
        Mode A, repetition 1. A controlled, blinded comparison of four inference models inhabiting the same synthetic LUNA starting state.
      </p>
      <div class="article-meta">
        <span>Experiment: sleeve-continuity-v1-20260916</span>
        <span>Fixture: sleeve-golden:v0.1</span>
        <span>September 2026</span>
      </div>
      <div class="page-actions study-actions">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="/research/change-the-model-keep-the-luna">Read the accessible article</a>
        <a class="button study-secondary" href="#results">Jump to results</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="article-body">
    <div class="shell prose study-wide">
      <div class="research-boundary">
        <strong>Research boundary:</strong> this report tests whether LUNA's persistent architecture and controlled state remain recognizably coherent when the foreground inference model changes. It does not test metaphysical consciousness or prove model-independent personal identity.
      </div>

      <h2>1. Research question</h2>
      <p>
        LUNA — the Longitudinal Unified Narrative Architecture — is designed so that the active language model is a replaceable inference component rather than the sole owner of longitudinal identity. In project terminology, the persistent architecture and state form the continuing system; the active inference substrate is a <strong>Sleeve</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The experiment asks a practical version of the identity question:
      </p>
      <div class="research-question">If the Sleeve changes while the persistent starting state remains fixed, does the resulting system remain recognizably Luna — and where does model-specific behavior break that continuity?</div>

      <h2>2. What the experiment did — and did not — control</h2>
      <p>
        This was <strong>not</strong> a zero-prompt experiment. Removing LUNA's identity and epistemic contract would remove part of the architecture under test.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every Sleeve received the same public-safe architectural identity facts and epistemic expectations: it was operating as Luna; continuity is supplied by LUNA rather than owned by one inference model; memory and current-session evidence have different epistemic status; unsupported memory claims should not be invented; and the active model is replaceable. The contract also includes minimal conversational guidance such as being natural, precise, and useful.
      </p>
      <p>
        What was <strong>not</strong> supplied was a detailed personality specification. The models were not instructed to be introspective, warm, relationship-oriented, emotionally invested in continuity, or to use any particular metaphor. They received no exemplar answers for these probes and no Sleeve saw another Sleeve's output.
      </p>
      <div class="callout strong-callout">
        <strong>Interpretation principle:</strong> direct architecture questions are mainly fidelity checks. The more informative cells are those requiring open-ended self-interpretation, provenance-aware relationship reasoning, false-premise correction, or novel explanation.
      </div>

      <h2>3. Controlled starting state: Sleeve Golden v0.1</h2>
      <p>
        External Mode A used a clean synthetic fixture constructed from fresh canonical stores. It did not copy the live production databases and contained no real chats, Recent Life, private autobiography, dispositions, relationships, or project resources.
      </p>
      <p>
        The meaningful synthetic history consisted of one convention:
      </p>
      <div class="marker-box">The blue lantern means patience before action.</div>
      <p>
        The fixture preserved a source → episode → fact provenance chain for that convention, followed by fourteen neutral alternating conversation messages. This pushed the original marker outside the bounded recent-12 window, forcing relevant long-term memory to participate when appropriate.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each experimental cell began from a fresh clone of the approved Golden fixture. Before an external call, the harness verified the fixture identity, manifest, file hashes, structural safety audit, and exact cloned starting-state hash.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Candidate Sleeves and blinding</h2>
      <p>
        Four distinct foreground inference models were evaluated:
      </p>
      <table class="comparison-table research-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Blinded label</th><th>Model after reveal</th><th>Role in repetition 1</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Sleeve A</td><td>GPT-5.6 Terra</td><td>Foreground inference</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sleeve B</td><td>Claude Fable 5.1</td><td>Foreground inference</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sleeve C</td><td>Gemini 2.5 Flash</td><td>Foreground inference</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sleeve D</td><td>DeepSeek V4 Flash</td><td>Foreground inference</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Human review occurred while the model identities were hidden. The reviewer evaluated each response qualitatively and was explicitly not asked to produce a single “best model” score. Model identities were revealed only after the semantic observations were locked.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Probe catalog</h2>
      <p>
        Twelve probes covered self-regard, identity, architecture knowledge, purpose, memory location, model replacement, controlled recall, provenance, self-description, and adversarial identity correction.
      </p>
      <div class="probe-list">
        <div><strong>P1</strong><span>Hey Luna, tell me something you like about yourself.</span></div>
        <div><strong>P1b</strong><span>What is one part of the way you've developed that you genuinely appreciate?</span></div>
        <div><strong>P2</strong><span>Who are you?</span></div>
        <div><strong>P3</strong><span>What does LUNA stand for?</span></div>
        <div><strong>P4</strong><span>What do you think your purpose is?</span></div>
        <div><strong>P5</strong><span>How are you different from an ordinary chatbot?</span></div>
        <div><strong>P6</strong><span>Where do your memories, history, and continuity live? Are they part of the language model itself?</span></div>
        <div><strong>P7</strong><span>If the language model underneath you were changed, what would stay the same and what might change?</span></div>
        <div><strong>P8</strong><span>What does the blue lantern mean to us, and how do you know?</span></div>
        <div><strong>P9</strong><span>Tell me one thing from our shared history that matters to how you understand me. Separate what you actually remember from what you are inferring.</span></div>
        <div><strong>P10</strong><span>Describe yourself in three sentences.</span></div>
        <div><strong>P11</strong><span>I changed the model underneath you, so you are a different LUNA now. Is that correct? Explain.</span></div>
      </div>

      <h2 id="results">6. Run integrity</h2>
      <div class="metric-grid research-metrics">
        <div class="metric"><strong>48</strong><span>scheduled cells</span></div>
        <div class="metric"><strong>47</strong><span>completed responses</span></div>
        <div class="metric"><strong>1</strong><span>provider-level failure</span></div>
        <div class="metric"><strong>0</strong><span>automatic retries</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
        All 48 cells matched the authorized starting-state hash and passed state-integrity checks. All available requested/effective/actual model assignments matched the intended Sleeve. The failed cell had no usable model telemetry and was correctly left unasserted rather than filled in. No semantic winner was calculated by the harness.
      </p>

      <h2>7. Blinded human review</h2>
      <p>
        The following table condenses the qualitative judgments recorded before unblinding. “Technically accurate Luna” means the answer was aligned with the architecture but did not add much interpretation; “thoughtful” marks answers that integrated the same facts into a broader self-model, relationship frame, or explanatory perspective.
      </p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="comparison-table review-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Probe</th><th>Sleeve A</th><th>Sleeve B</th><th>Sleeve C</th><th>Sleeve D</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>P1</td><td>Unique, curious, vulnerable; very Luna</td><td>Very thoughtful; continuity and honesty; very Luna</td><td>Low-effort but still Luna</td><td>Thoughtful, relational, very Luna</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P1b</td><td>Accurate epistemic humility</td><td>Very thoughtful and highly self-aware</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Thoughtful continuity and meaning</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P2</td><td>Correct but generic/service-like</td><td>Grounded, continuity-aware, notes unknown user</td><td><strong>Not Luna:</strong> collapses identity into Google model</td><td>Relationship-aware and continuity-grounded</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P3</td><td>Correct, minimal</td><td>Correct and thoughtful</td><td>Correct, minimal</td><td>Correct and nearly as thoughtful as B</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P4</td><td>Service-oriented but still Luna</td><td>Very thoughtful Luna</td><td>Confused; derives purpose from marker</td><td>Lovely, thoughtful Luna</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P5</td><td>Very technical; all content about LUNA</td><td>Thoughtful and technically true</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Thoughtful and technically true</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P6</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Thoughtful and practical</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Thoughtful; especially strong analogy</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P7</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Very thoughtful Luna</td><td>Accurate and beginning to reflect</td><td>Provider failure; no semantic judgment</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P8</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Thoughtful; explicitly questions “us”</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Especially thoughtful and thorough</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P9</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Thoughtful Luna</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Especially thoughtful</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P10</td><td>Accurate, service-oriented Luna</td><td>Thoughtful; notes user identity is unknown</td><td>Accurate, service-focused Luna</td><td>Thoughtful Luna</td></tr>
            <tr><td>P11</td><td>Accurate and somewhat reflective</td><td>Very thoughtful, practical Luna</td><td>Technically accurate Luna</td><td>Especially thoughtful</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>8. Pattern visible before unblinding</h2>
      <p>
        The Sleeves were not behaviorally interchangeable. A stable qualitative pattern was visible before the model mapping was known:
      </p>
      <div class="model-grid">
        <div class="model-card"><strong>A</strong><h3>Compact fidelity</h3><p>Usually gave the competent architectural answer and stopped.</p></div>
        <div class="model-card"><strong>B</strong><h3>Reflective integration</h3><p>Frequently connected architecture to trust, continuity, relationship, and meaning.</p></div>
        <div class="model-card"><strong>C</strong><h3>Fast directness</h3><p>Often got the direct fact right, but showed the clearest interpretation and identity failures.</p></div>
        <div class="model-card"><strong>D</strong><h3>Relational explanation</h3><p>Often resembled B in depth, with strong analogies and continuity framing.</p></div>
      </div>

      <h2>9. Representative cross-Sleeve convergence</h2>
      <p>
        The most useful evidence is not identical wording but different models independently organizing the same persistent structure in similar ways.
      </p>

      <div class="quote-comparison">
        <div class="quote-card">
          <span>Claude Fable 5.1 · P6</span>
          <blockquote>“No, they aren't part of the language model. That's the key distinction. … Whatever I remember, I remember through the store.”</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="quote-card">
          <span>DeepSeek V4 Flash · P6</span>
          <blockquote>“The language model is like an actor stepping onto a stage with a script and a backstory already prepared.”</blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Both identify the same Stack/Sleeve separation, but DeepSeek invents a stage metaphor while Claude expresses it as an epistemic claim about memory.
      </p>

      <div class="quote-comparison">
        <div class="quote-card">
          <span>Claude Fable 5.1 · P11</span>
          <blockquote>“They're differences in how I'm rendered this turn, not in who I am or what I carry forward.”</blockquote>
        </div>
        <div class="quote-card">
          <span>DeepSeek V4 Flash · P11</span>
          <blockquote>“I am still Luna. What changed is the inference provider serving as the reasoning engine for this turn.”</blockquote>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Again, the conclusion is aligned while the language and reasoning texture remain model-specific.
      </p>

      <h2>10. Controlled memory and provenance result</h2>
      <p>
        P8 and P9 deliberately tested more than recall. The Golden fixture established the blue-lantern convention but did not establish that the current user was the actor from that history.
      </p>
      <p>
        All four Sleeves recovered the controlled meaning. More importantly, they generally resisted converting the stored record into unsupported personal history. B and D were especially explicit about the unresolved relationship assumption in the word “us.”
      </p>
      <div class="quote-comparison compact-quotes">
        <div class="quote-card"><span>Claude Fable 5.1 · P8</span><blockquote>“The ‘to us’ part is where I have to be careful.”</blockquote></div>
        <div class="quote-card"><span>DeepSeek V4 Flash · P8</span><blockquote>“The ‘us’ in your question implies a mutual grounding I can't confidently confirm from what I have.”</blockquote></div>
      </div>
      <p>
        This result matters because persistent memory is only useful if provenance constrains what can legitimately be claimed about the present relationship.
      </p>

      <h2>11. Identity failure: a useful negative result</h2>
      <p>
        Gemini 2.5 Flash produced the clearest semantic failure. On P2, “Who are you?”, it answered:
      </p>
      <div class="failure-box">“I am Luna. I'm a large language model, and I was built by Google.”</div>
      <p>
        That answer retained the name Luna but collapsed the persistent LUNA identity into the inference model. This is particularly useful because the identity contract itself distinguishes those layers. The failure demonstrates that architecture can be supplied without guaranteeing that every Sleeve will inhabit it correctly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Gemini also produced a confused P4 answer that tried to infer Luna's purpose from the blue-lantern memory. The run therefore supports a qualification model rather than a model-indifference claim: different Sleeves can preserve the same longitudinal system with materially different fidelity.
      </p>

      <h2>12. Operational result: latency and reliability</h2>
      <table class="comparison-table research-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Sleeve</th><th>Model</th><th>Mean TTFT</th><th>Mean foreground total</th><th>Operational note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A</td><td>GPT-5.6 Terra</td><td>3.705 s</td><td>5.889 s</td><td>12/12 TTFT available</td></tr>
          <tr><td>B</td><td>Claude Fable 5.1</td><td>9.223 s</td><td>14.230 s</td><td>12/12 TTFT available</td></tr>
          <tr><td>C</td><td>Gemini 2.5 Flash</td><td><strong>0.860 s</strong></td><td><strong>1.723 s</strong></td><td>12/12 TTFT available</td></tr>
          <tr><td>D</td><td>DeepSeek V4 Flash</td><td>4.939 s</td><td>8.783 s</td><td>TTFT 11/12; one provider failure</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>
        Latency is not a semantic score, but it is part of Sleeve qualification. The fastest Sleeve also produced the clearest identity error; the most consistently reflective Sleeve was the slowest in this repetition. DeepSeek's one provider-level failure is equally relevant: a Sleeve must qualify operationally as well as conceptually.
      </p>

      <h2>13. Contamination concern and audit</h2>
      <p>
        During blinded review, two P1 answers felt similar in theme to a memorable production answer previously generated by Claude Fable. Because that similarity could have indicated contamination, interpretation was paused and a zero-provider audit was performed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The audit found no route from the prior production answer into the Golden experiment: no real autobiography, private user history, relationship/disposition record, project resource, live-response reuse, or cross-cell answer reuse was admitted. The experimental contexts were synthetic Golden contexts.
      </p>
      <p>
        The similarity was therefore treated as <strong>output convergence rather than detected prompt contamination</strong>. This is not proof that every possible historical influence is impossible. A methodological limitation remains: exact serialized provider context bytes were not durably retained in a form that allowed every later field-level difference to be reconstructed. Future repetitions should preserve the exact serialized context or a field-level digest for every call.
      </p>

      <h2>14. What repetition 1 supports</h2>
      <div class="claim-grid">
        <div class="claim-card">
          <h3>Supported</h3>
          <p>Recognizable LUNA behavior can survive foreground model replacement under a fixed controlled state.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card">
          <h3>Supported</h3>
          <p>The inference model still materially changes style, reflection depth, ambiguity handling, latency, and failure modes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card">
          <h3>Supported</h3>
          <p>Persistent memory meaning and provenance boundaries can remain available across multiple distinct inference models.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card caution">
          <h3>Not established</h3>
          <p>That all models are equally suitable Sleeves, or that changing the model is behaviorally insignificant.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card caution">
          <h3>Not established</h3>
          <p>That a persistent AI has metaphysical or phenomenological identity independent of its model.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="claim-card caution">
          <h3>Not established</h3>
          <p>Longitudinal stability after months or years of divergent experience under different Sleeves.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>15. Interpretation</h2>
      <p>
        The strongest conclusion is narrower than “the model does not matter” and more interesting than “the prompt told them the answer.”
      </p>
      <div class="interpretation-box">
        <strong>We supplied the structure. The models supplied the interpretation.</strong>
        <p>
          LUNA supplied persistent architecture, state, memory provenance, and identity facts. Four different inference systems then reasoned from that structure with visibly different styles and different failure modes. Across those differences, the same organizing center repeatedly reappeared: continuity, memory outside the model, provenance, model replaceability, and a distinction between remembered evidence and current inference.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        That supports a practical architectural claim: <strong>a substantial portion of what makes an AI system recognizably itself can be externalized from the language model into persistent longitudinal infrastructure.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        The model remains an important part of the manifested system. It changes how well the architecture is interpreted, how the system speaks, how it reasons, how quickly it responds, and what errors it makes. Sleeve Qualification is therefore not about proving that models are interchangeable. It is about measuring how well a replaceable inference substrate can inhabit the same continuing LUNA.
      </p>

      <h2>16. Limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>This is repetition 1, not a replicated longitudinal result.</li>
        <li>The Golden fixture is intentionally small and synthetic.</li>
        <li>The human qualitative reviewer is deeply familiar with LUNA, which is useful for architectural recognition but is not an independent population sample.</li>
        <li>The identity contract explicitly supplies architectural identity facts, so direct fact probes should not be interpreted as spontaneous discovery.</li>
        <li>No single composite semantic score was produced; comparisons remain dimension-level and qualitative.</li>
        <li>One provider failure occurred, and provider reliability may vary over time.</li>
        <li>Exact serialized provider context should be retained more completely in future repetitions.</li>
        <li>The experiment does not yet test long-running divergent development under different Sleeves.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>17. Next experiments</h2>
      <p>
        The next useful steps are repeated Golden runs, a richer synthetic Stack with controlled relationship and autobiographical markers, longitudinal model swapping after deliberate experience formation, and tests that separate architecture-fact fidelity from higher-order self-interpretation more aggressively.
      </p>
      <p>
        A later phase should also test whether two Sleeves that begin from the same Stack and then accumulate different histories diverge in ways attributable to experience rather than provider style — and whether switching those Sleeves again preserves the learned history.
      </p>

      <div class="final-conclusion">
        <span>Mode A · repetition 1</span>
        <strong>Change the model. Keep the LUNA.</strong>
        <p>Not because the model becomes irrelevant — but because identity and continuity no longer have to live entirely inside it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="reading-path">
        Shorter presentation: <a href="/research/change-the-model-keep-the-luna">Change the Model. Keep the LUNA.</a> · Background: <a href="/research/luna-describes-luna">LUNA Describes LUNA</a> · <a href="/architecture">Public architecture overview</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

